Newer than your training data may be. When these conflict with the documentation results above or with what you remember, prefer the most recent dated source.

{"content":{"result":[{"rank_order":1,"title":"AWS launches customer carbon footprint tool","context":"AWS has launched a new customer carbon footprint tool that enables customers to calculate the environmental impact of their AWS workloads. The tool provides historical carbon emissions data, emission trend evaluations, estimated carbon savings, and forecasted emissions based on current usage. It is accessible through the AWS Billing console and offers data visualizations by geographical location and AWS services like EC2 and S3","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2022/03/aws-launches-customer-carbon-footprint-tool/"},{"rank_order":2,"title":"Amazon S3 Storage Lens is now available in AWS GovCloud (US) Regions - AWS","context":"Amazon S3 Storage Lens, a tool that offers organization-wide visibility into S3 storage usage and activity, is now available in AWS GovCloud (US) Regions. This expansion brings metrics to help optimize storage costs, identify data protection opportunities, and enhance application performance. S3 Storage Lens provides a unified view of object storage usage and activity across numerous accounts, with drill-downs to generate insights at various aggregation levels. Users can optimize storage costs by detecting incomplete multipart uploads or buckets with non-current object versions. They can also identify buckets not adhering to data protection best practices, such as using S3 Cross-Region Replication or S3 Versioning. The newly added performance metrics enable users to detect application performance constraints, like inefficient access patterns or cross-Region data transfer, to minimize latency and costs. S3 Storage Lens is accessible in all AWS Regions and comes with free metrics for all customers and 14 days of historical data. For more detailed visibility and up to 15 months data retention, users can upgrade to S3 Storage Lens advanced metrics. For more information on S3 Storage Lens metrics and pricing, refer to the documentation and Amazon S3 pricing page, respectively","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2026/01/s3-storage-lens-aws-govcloud-us-regions/"},{"rank_order":3,"title":"querying-aws-s3","skill_description":"Queries S3 object metadata, tracks bucket activity, audits object changes, searches annotations, and analyzes storage metrics using S3 Metadata system tables (journal, inventory, annotation) and S3 Storage Lens tables via Athena SQL. Applies when counting objects, finding recent uploads or deletions, identifying who wrote to a prefix, breaking down storage classes, finding objects by tag, searching annotation content, analyzing storage lens metrics, or enabling S3 Metadata tracking. Prefers system tables over raw S3 APIs (list-objects-v2, head-object) at scale. Trigger phrases: bucket activity, object count, who uploaded, track deletions, storage class breakdown, find by tag, search annotations, storage lens metrics, audit bucket changes.\n\nServices: s3, s3tables, athena, glue\nTasks: debug, audit, configure\nPersona: developer, data-engineer, devops\nWorkload: data-analytics, observability\n*","skill_name":"querying-aws-s3"},{"rank_order":4,"title":"querying-aws-cloudwatch","skill_description":"Runs SQL queries on CloudWatch Logs data exported as Apache Iceberg tables in S3 Tables. Covers VPC Flow Logs, WAF logs, CloudFront access logs, Route 53 resolver logs, Network Firewall logs, EKS audit logs, Verified Access logs, SES logs, VPC Lattice logs, Step Functions logs, NLB access logs, S3 Server Access Logs, and 20+ other AWS vended data sources. Applies when analyzing network traffic, investigating security incidents, querying exported logs with SQL, enabling S3 Tables integration, configuring log export, correlating logs with other data, running Athena queries on the aws-cloudwatch table bucket, analyzing S3 access patterns, S3 latency investigation, S3 cost attribution, or bucket access auditing.\n\nServices: cloudwatch, s3tables, athena, glue, s3\nTasks: build, deploy, debug, audit, configure\nPersona: developer, data-engineer, devops\nWorkload: data-analytics, observability\n*","skill_name":"querying-aws-cloudwatch"},{"rank_order":5,"title":"ingesting-into-data-lake","skill_description":"Import data into the AWS data lake from S3 files, local uploads, JDBC databases (Oracle, SQL Server, PostgreSQL, MySQL, RDS, Aurora), Amazon Redshift, Snowflake, BigQuery, DynamoDB, or existing Glue catalog tables (migration). Default target is S3 Tables; standard Iceberg on a general purpose bucket is supported where S3 Tables is not adopted. Handles one-time loads, recurring pipelines, migrations. Triggers on: import data, load data, ingest, sync database, migrate table, move data to AWS, set up pipeline, ETL, pull from Snowflake, query BigQuery into S3, export DynamoDB, CTAS, convert to Iceberg. Do NOT use for setting up or troubleshooting Glue connections (use connecting-to-data-source), creating empty tables (use creating-data-lake-table), running queries (use querying-data-lake), finding tables by fuzzy name (use finding-data-lake-assets), catalog audit (use exploring-data-catalog), or SaaS platforms like Salesforce, ServiceNow, SAP, MongoDB, Kafka.\n\n\nServices: glue, s3, s3tables, athena, dynamodb\nTasks: deploy, migrate\nPersona: developer, data-engineer\nWorkload: data-analytics\n*","skill_name":"ingesting-into-data-lake"}]}}"}]}], "label": "Look up AWS docs on Customer Carbon Footprint Tool reporting lag and storage class impact"}
